= Conclusion and Future Work <sec-conclusion>

The `desmet` harness answers RQ1 in the affirmative: five platforms with five different orchestration models---a compiled state graph, a sequential crew, a manager-driven team, a handoff chain, and a sequential-loop composition---executed the same four-stage pipeline behind one adapter contract, with the same prompts, tools, model, and scoring, and produced comparable per-stage metrics from instrumentation surfaces as different as response usage fields, an event-bus interceptor, and a chat middleware. Adding a platform is one method and a registry entry.

On RQ2, holding the model constant made framework behaviour visible that model benchmarks cannot show: a ~2.4× token-cost spread on identical work; conversation-history policy, not architectural category, as its predictor; a redundant-tool-call rate that flags the stages where retry policy, not the task, is burning budget; and three fault classes that appeared only under a second model and provider. Two of the harness's own defences---the productive-tool-call guard and the Magentic round cap---were themselves consequences of what the runs revealed.

Three extensions are specified and scaffolded. First, running the implemented visual-platform adapters end-to-end, so that the comparison spans all three architectural categories. Second, running the intermediate and advanced scenarios, which is the only way to decide whether CrewAI's and MAF's cost profiles are a fixed per-turn surcharge or a compounding history-replay cost. Third, repeated runs with the harness's variance metrics and a second evaluator for the manual dimension, to put confidence bounds on the baseline and an inter-rater statistic on the rubric. Beyond these, the model-sensitivity signal of @tab-sensitivity could be promoted to a fifth, scored _Model Robustness_ dimension once at least three model conditions are available to calibrate it.
